# Why a fiber did not end normally (spec 11.5): an error (`Fail`), a defect (`Die`) or an
# interruption (`Interrupt`).
#
# A value of "one of the errors in E" cannot be written (spec 7.1), so a `Cause[E]` does not give
# out its error. Look at its kind, show it, or raise it again with `Cause.raise` and catch the
# error with `escape`.
#
# The functions are built in: the runtime makes and reads causes (NOTES.md).

## The cause of a fiber's failure. `E` is the error set it can fail with.
##
## The runtime holds the value. The field only makes `E` an error set (spec 9.2); it is never
## read.
pub opaque type Cause[E](raise: fn() -> Never fails E)

## The three kinds of causes.
pub enum CauseKind {
  ## An error raised with `fail`. Shows up in `E`.
  Fail
  ## A defect (spec 7.5). Does not show up in the type.
  Die
  ## An interruption (spec 11.4). Does not show up in the type.
  Interrupt
}
  derive Eq, Show

## The kind of the cause.
@intrinsic
pub fn kind[E](c: Cause[E]) -> CauseKind

## The cause as text: `error: ...`, `defect: ...` or `interrupted`.
@intrinsic
pub fn to_string[E](c: Cause[E]) -> String

## Raises the cause again: `Fail` fails with the error, `Die` is a defect, and `Interrupt`
## unwinds as an interruption.
@intrinsic
pub fn raise[A, E](c: Cause[E]) -> A fails E
